<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: The Americas before Columbus</h1><p class="ws-lede">Great cities, empires and roads built long before 1492.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/the-americas-before-columbus">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 5 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Inca Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Aztec Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Teotihuacan</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Atahualpa</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Moctezuma II</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Teotihuacan</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;A city of a hundred thousand people, planned on a grid, that shaped Mesoamerica centuries before the Aztecs.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 4, <b>Moctezuma II</b>: &#8220;The emperor who received Cortes, and whose fate still divides historians.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 7, <b>Atahualpa</b>: &#8220;The Inca who won a civil war just in time to meet Pizarro at Cajamarca.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;Great cities, empires and roads built long before 1492.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/the-americas-before-columbus. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: The Americas before Columbus</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Teotihuacan <span>from 100 BCE</span></li><li>Aztec Empire <span>from 1325 CE</span></li><li>Inca Empire <span>from 1438 CE</span></li><li>Moctezuma II <span>born c. 1466 CE</span></li><li>Atahualpa <span>born c. 1502 CE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
